.TH @APP@ 1 "@DATE@" "odox @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@APP@ \- @SUMMARY@
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @APP@
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
@DESCRIPTION@
.PP
The document is read from the file and nothing is sent anywhere. In edit mode
text is typed and formatted, paragraphs are made headings and list items, and
tables, pictures, notes, comments, links and fields are added in a text
document; values and formulas are typed into cells and cells formatted, sorted
and filled in a spreadsheet; shapes are moved, resized and typed into, and
slides, text boxes and pictures added, in a presentation. Every change can be
undone. What was not touched is written back as it was read, element for
element, so the saved document is the opened one with the changes in it. The
document is written only on Save, to the file that was opened or the one
named; before it is, what is about to be written is read back and compared with
the document in the window, and a difference refuses the save. A document can
also be exported as PDF/UA or printed, and spelling is checked in edit mode
against the system's Hunspell dictionaries.
.SH OPTIONS
There are none. A path given on the command line is opened at startup; a
document may also be chosen with Ctrl+O.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Open a document.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Read the open document from disk again.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
Close it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Shift+S
Save over the open file; save somewhere else.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F, F3, Shift+F3
Search the document; go to the next match; go to the one before.
.TP
.B Ctrl+H
Search and replace, where the document can be changed.
.TP
.B Ctrl+P
Print the document.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
In a text document or on a slide, make the selection a link, or change the
link the caret is in.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V
In a spreadsheet, copy, cut and paste the selected cells as tab separated text.
.TP
.B F5, Shift+F5
In a presentation, start the slideshow at the first slide; at the one in view.
Escape ends it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z
Take an edit back; put it back.
.TP
.B Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I, Ctrl+U
In a text document or on a slide, make the selection bold, italic or
underlined, or take it off where all of it is; with nothing selected, what is
typed next.
.TP
.B Ctrl+E
Edit mode on or off. A window opens reading unless the preference in the Edit
menu says otherwise.
.TP
.B Ctrl+plus, Ctrl+minus, Ctrl+0
Zoom in, zoom out, and back to actual size, which is the printed size. Ctrl
with the mouse wheel zooms too. A text document opens fitted to the width of
the window and a presentation to the whole window, until the zoom is changed
by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/odox/settings.toml
The one preference, whether a document opens in edit mode, written only when it
is changed in the menu.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/odox/recent-@APP@
The paths of the last ten documents @APP@ opened or saved, newest first, one to
a line, for File > Open recent.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/odox/words.txt
The words added to the dictionary from the spell check, one to a line.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/odox/recovery/
A copy of a document's unsaved changes, kept every minute while there are any
and taken away when they are saved or discarded; after a crash, the next window
started without a file offers to open it.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/hunspell/
Hunspell dictionaries of the user's own, read before the system's in
.IR /usr/share/hunspell .
.PP
.I ~/.config
and
.I ~/.local/share
stand in where
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
and
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
are unset. A save goes through a
.I .part
file beside the target that is renamed over it, and a print through a PDF in
the temporary directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xodt (1),
.BR xods (1),
.BR xodp (1)
.PP
Each reads one kind of OpenDocument file and names its sibling when handed
another.
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson.
